    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d399">

                <pb facs="0480" n="468" sameAs="frus:frus1941v02/pg468"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/16797<hi
                        rendition="#superscript">1/6</hi>: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-11-20T12:00:00+01:00">November 20,
                            1941—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received November 20—8:45 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>5547. Personal from Former Naval Person<anchor n="19" xml:id="d399-f1"/> to the
                    President.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <list>
                        <item>“2. It would be disastrous if Weygand were to be replaced by some
                            pro-Hun officer just at the moment when we are likely to be in a
                            position to influence events in North Africa both from the East and from
                            home. I hope you will try your utmost at Vichy to preserve Weygand in
                            his command. If this cannot be achieved some friendly figure from
                            retirement, like General Georges might be agreed upon. I have not seen
                            Georges since the collapse but I have reason to believe his heart is
                            sound. I knew him very well. Anyhow, Mr. President, Tunis and all French
                            North Africa might open out to us if we gain a good victory in Libya and
                            we must be ready to exploit success. I am afraid, on the other hand,
                            lest Hitler may demand to occupy Bizerta in view of possible danger to
                            Tripoli. It is now or never with the Vichy French and their last chance
                            of redemption.</item>
                        <item>3. Very glad to get Averell<anchor n="20" xml:id="d399-f2"/> back
                            here.”</item>
                    </list>
                </quote>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d399-f1" n="19" xml:id="d399-f1-content">
                <p>Code name for Winston S. Churchill, British Prime Minister.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d399-f2" n="20" xml:id="d399-f2-content">
                <p>W. Averell Harriman, special representative of President Roosevelt in the United
                    Kingdom, with the rank of Minister, with responsibility for expediting
                    lend-lease aid to the British Empire.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
